title:: MIRa and FluCoMa
summary:: Choosing complementary SuperCollider analysis tools
categories:: Libraries>MIR
related:: Guides/MIRa, Guides/MIRa-legacy-migration

MIRa is designed to complement FluCoMa, not reproduce its entire feature inventory.

section:: Prefer FluCoMa

FluCoMa is the stronger starting point for corpus and dataset workflows, PCA and dimensionality reduction, clustering, NMF, HPSS, novelty/transient slicing, and modern Mel/MFCC pipelines where migration from historical SC analyzers is irrelevant.

section:: Prefer MIRa

MIRa is useful when a SynthDef needs a precisely documented successor to legacy code::Pitch::, code::MFCC::, spectral statistics, flux, or onset analysis; when YIN, MPM or SWIPE must be selected explicitly; or when periodicity, voicing, confidence, and octave ambiguity should become separate control signals.

The current unpublished development expansion also provides paper-based HPCP, tuning, CENS, Tonnetz, harmonic descriptors, tempograms, wavelet analysis/reconstruction, auditory filter banks, constant-Q transforms, PNCC and richer pitch evidence. Each class documents its online adaptations and limits; implementation coverage does not imply general recognition accuracy.

section:: Interoperation

Both projects output normal server control signals, so MIRa evidence can drive FluCoMa slicing or dataset capture, and FluCoMa-derived features can be combined with MIRa pitch or onset controls in the same SynthDef. Compare update rates and normalize deliberately; similarly named confidence values are not automatically equivalent.
